---
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import Draft from '../../components/Draft.astro';
import Provider from '../../components/Provider.astro';
import { entity } from '../../lib/entity';

const changed = '2026-11-08';
---

<StarlightPage frontmatter={{ title: 'Terms of service', description: 'The terms version.cam is provided under.' }}>
	<Draft />
	<p class="vc-lead">
		These terms apply to anyone who uses the hosted service at version.cam, app.version.cam, api.version.cam and
		clips.version.cam ("you"). The versioncam package on npm and the GitHub Action are under their own licence,
		the Functional Source License, and these terms do not change it. The service is provided by:
	</p>
	<Provider />

	<h2>Who may sign up</h2>
	<p>
		You sign up through a GitHub account, as a business or as a person who is at least 18 years old. If you sign
		up for an organisation, you confirm that you may bind it. The service is made for businesses; where you use
		it as a consumer, the law of your country gives you rights these terms do not take away.
	</p>

	<h2>The service</h2>
	<p>
		The service stores the recordings your CI publishes with <code>versioncam publish</code>, renders them, serves
		them at addresses that play the latest version, tells you when a clip stops matching your app, and reports on
		your pull requests through the Versioncam GitHub App. It never records your app itself: your CI does, with
		your own credentials, which never reach us.
	</p>

	<h2>The beta</h2>
	<p>
		While the beta is private, we do not sell plans. We give Pro by hand to the accounts that ask, as the{' '}
		<a href="/#after-ci-versioncam">front page</a> says, and these terms apply to a plan we gave exactly as they
		apply to one that is paid for.
	</p>

	<h2>Your account</h2>
	<p>
		You sign in with GitHub. Who may see and manage a project follows from GitHub: whoever may read a repository
		where the Versioncam App is installed sees its project, whoever administers the repository can change it. You are
		responsible for who has access to your GitHub accounts, and for any project token or command-line token you
		make.
	</p>

	<h2>Your content</h2>
	<p>
		The recordings, their screens and the files rendered from them are yours. You give us the right to store,
		render and show them as your project's settings say (public, unlisted or private) and for no other purpose.
		You confirm that you may record and publish what your recordings show; if they show personal data, you are
		its controller and we process it for you under the <a href="/legal/dpa/">data processing terms</a>, which
		are part of these terms.
	</p>

	<h2 id="not-allowed">What may not be hosted</h2>
	<p>
		The service is for recordings of software you may record and publish. Not allowed on clips.version.cam or
		anywhere in the service: content that is illegal under the law of Slovenia or of the European Union; content
		that infringes someone else's rights, such as copyright, a trade mark or their privacy; recordings that show
		people you have no right to show; malware, or links to it; and files that are not recordings of your own
		product. Also not allowed: reaching for other customers' data, overloading the service on purpose, and
		getting around its limits. We decide what to remove by hand, never by an automated system.
	</p>

	<h2 id="reporting">Reporting a clip</h2>
	<p>
		If you believe a clip on clips.version.cam is illegal or breaks the rule above, write to{' '}
		<a href={`mailto:${entity.contactEmail}`}>{entity.contactEmail}</a> with: the clip's address; why you believe
		it is illegal or breaks the rule, naming the law or the right you rely on where you can; your name and e-mail
		address, unless the content concerns a sexual-abuse offence, where you may stay anonymous; and a sentence
		saying you believe in good faith that your report is accurate and complete. We confirm that it arrived, look
		at the clip, decide without undue delay and in any case within fourteen days, and tell you what we decided and
		why (Regulation (EU) 2022/2065, Article 16).
	</p>
	<p>
		If we remove or restrict a clip, we tell the project's owner what was removed, why, which rule or law it broke,
		and how to object: by writing to the same address, where we answer the objection the same way (Article 17).
	</p>

	<h2>Plans and payment</h2>
	<p>
		The plans, what each one stores and sends, and their prices are on the <a href="/#after-ci-versioncam">front
		page</a> and in <a href="/more/version-cam/">the docs</a>; the price at checkout is the price. Paid plans are
		billed in advance, monthly or yearly, through Stripe; prices exclude VAT, which is added where it applies. A
		subscription renews until you cancel it in the billing portal; cancelling takes effect at the end of the paid
		period. We tell you at least 30 days before a price changes, and a change never applies to a period you have
		already paid for. Refunds follow the <a href="/legal/refunds/">refund policy</a>.
	</p>

	<h2>When a plan ends</h2>
	<p>
		Your clips keep playing where they are embedded. The plans say what a lapsed plan no longer covers: we stop
		storing new recordings of private projects, since Free covers public repositories only, and we keep versions older than the free
		plan's history for sixty more days, send two reminders, and then remove them.
	</p>

	<h2>Deleting</h2>
	<p>
		Deleting a project in its settings removes its recordings, renders and history at once, and its files within
		minutes. The database keeps thirty days of point-in-time history, so a record deleted by mistake can be
		restored on request within thirty days; a deleted file cannot. You can download any version as a zip before
		you delete it.
	</p>

	<h2>Availability</h2>
	<p>
		We work to keep the service available and your data safe, but we do not promise it will never be interrupted,
		except where a separate agreement says so. Your recordings also remain in your own CI, so the service is never
		the only copy of your work.
	</p>

	<h2>Liability</h2>
	<p>
		The service is provided as it is. To the extent the law allows, we are not liable for indirect or
		consequential losses, and our total liability is limited to what you paid us in the twelve months before the
		claim. Nothing in these terms limits liability that the law does not allow to be limited, or the rights a
		consumer has under the law of their country.
	</p>

	<h2>Ending the agreement</h2>
	<p>
		You may stop using the service and delete your projects at any time. We may suspend an account that breaks
		these terms, telling you why and how to object, and end it if the breach is not put right.
	</p>

	<h2>Law, and changes</h2>
	<p>
		These terms are governed by the law of Slovenia, and its courts decide disputes, except that a consumer may
		also rely on the law and the courts of their own country. We may change these terms with 30 days' notice to
		the e-mail address we have for your account; the version in force is always this page, last changed on{' '}
		{changed}.
	</p>

	<p>Questions: <a href={`mailto:${entity.contactEmail}`}>{entity.contactEmail}</a>.</p>
</StarlightPage>
